{
  "title": "Port the server to SQLite and cut prod over (step 2 of the SQLite pilot)",
  "description": "doc/sqlite-pilot-analysis.md is the step-1 pre-analysis: it lists every Postgres-specific feature with its SQLite equivalent, has benchmarks from scripts/sqlite-pilot-bench.py, and recommends going ahead as a cutover rather than keeping two backends permanently. \
If the owner says go: add a SQLite backend (pool with WAL/busy_timeout/foreign_keys pragmas and app-level write serialization, a baseline SQLite migration, and uuid/JSON column newtypes, with an id generated in Rust on every New* insert struct, since most rely on Postgres's gen_random_uuid() default today); port the search filter to an FTS5 trigram index over current versions, with text pre-normalized by ramekin_core::search::normalize_for_search (not FTS5 remove_diacritics, which misses the contract's ligature expansions) and a normalize_for_search UDF fallback for tokens under 3 characters, and make the search-normalization contract vectors pass against SQLite; replace the pg_current_xact_id sync cursors with a sync_clock counter (coordinate with blocked-one-sync-mechanism; plus rejecting cursors above the current clock with a cursorless full sync, which also fixes p3-sync-cursor-after-restore, including restores of older backups); move recipe_versions.photo_ids off uuid[]; and replace citext tag names and lower(username) lookups with Rust-normalized key columns (Unicode case folding, which SQLite lacks). \
Run the e2e suite against both backends in CI until SQLite passes, write and rehearse the data migration from the doc's outline (keep soft-deleted rows, seed sync_clock from the Postgres pg_snapshot_xmax captured at the freeze, so it starts above every client's persisted cursor, then restamp every current version and live tag above it so delta syncs re-deliver the new ingredient haystack), cut prod over, then delete the Postgres backend.",
